  "content": {
    "text": "96\n\n Good Firms vs. Good Deals \u2013 They\u2019re Not the Same (and Why That Matters)\nHi Sara,\nIf you\u2019re on the hunt for an accounting firm to buy, you\u2019ve probably caught yourself scrolling listings like it\u2019s late-night Zillow\u2014dreaming of that perfect cloud-based beauty with remote staff and sky-high margins.\u00a0\nBut here\u2019s a little reality check: the \u201cperfect\u201d firm doesn\u2019t always mean the \u201cperfect\u201d deal. And if you mix those up? You could end up with a cash flow nightmare instead of your next big win.\u00a0\nLet\u2019s break down why terms matter just as much as the firm\u2014and how knowing the difference could save you from overpaying your way into buyer\u2019s remorse.\nI spent most of my week talking to buyers\u2014and when Letter of Intent season is in full swing, you know it feels a lot like musical chairs:\nTax season slows things down.\n\nMay and June? Buyers flood in, offers fly.\n\nJuly and August? Closings heat up.\n\nSeptember 15 & October 15 deadlines creep up\u2014panic.\n\nThen it\u2019s holidays, and boom, back to tax season.\n\nWhen this happens, buyers are scanning listings, making snap judgments:\n\u2705 Cloud-based firm, great margins? Good deal.\n\u274c Local, paper-based, not remote? Bad deal.\nExcept\u2026 that\u2019s not how this works.\nA good firm doesn\u2019t always equal a good deal.\nWhy? Because a good deal is the firm + the terms.\nYou need both to make the math work:\nGood firm, bad terms? Overpay, and it won\u2019t cash flow. You\u2019re toast.\n\nLess desirable firm, great terms? Might be your best deal yet.\n\nI hear this all the time: \u201cBut this firm is perfect\u2014remote team, owner works on the business, not in it.\u201d\n(Yes, we should all drink every time someone says that.)\nBut if you\u2019re paying 10x earnings? That\u2019s not a dream. That\u2019s a future bankruptcy filing.\nOn the flip side, a \u201cmeh\u201d firm\u2014local clients, paper-based\u2014might be a home run if you negotiate seller financing, a lower price, or better transition support.\nMy grandmother used to say: There\u2019s a lid for every pot.\nAnd in this market, there\u2019s a deal structure that can make almost any firm a win\u2014if you know how to write your LOI and set the right terms.\nSo, if you\u2019re hunting for the perfect firm\u2026 maybe what you really need is the perfect deal structure.\nIf you want help making that happen, check out Deal Academy, where I break this all down.\nCheers (and to smart deals),\nSarah Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nFounder, Deal Academy\nP.S. If you're looking to buy or sell an accounting firm, and want me to keep an eye out for you, fill out this form here.\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
